# =============================================================================
# labwc autostart — what runs once the window manager is up
# =============================================================================
# labwc runs this file as a shell script after it has read rc.xml and the
# `environment` file next to this one. By that point WAYLAND_DISPLAY exists,
# which is why the shell can only be started from here and not from
# /usr/bin/aquarius-session.
#
# NO MACHINE-SPECIFIC PATHS IN THIS FILE. The shell is started with a bare `qs`,
# which finds its configuration through QS_CONFIG_PATH, exported by
# /usr/bin/aquarius-session before labwc was launched. Same for $AQ_LOG.
#
# (This file is the AquariusOS copy of the one in the aquarius-shell repository.
# The niri equivalent of every line here is in that repository's
# session/niri/config.kdl, which AquariusOS does not ship.)
# =============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Tell systemd and D-Bus that a screen now exists.
#
# The launcher already pushed XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP and friends across, but
# WAYLAND_DISPLAY did not exist yet — labwc had not started. Anything launched
# on demand from now on (the portals, the keyring, a Flatpak's file dialog)
# needs it, and a portal that does not know which Wayland display to talk to
# fails silently rather than loudly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if command -v dbus-update-activation-environment > /dev/null 2>&1; then
    dbus-update-activation-environment --systemd WAYLAND_DISPLAY DISPLAY 2>/dev/null || true
else
    systemctl --user import-environment WAYLAND_DISPLAY DISPLAY 2>/dev/null || true
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Tell systemd the graphical session has begun.
#
# Portals, notification daemons and keyring agents are systemd user services
# that say "start me with graphical-session.target". labwc ships
# labwc-session.target, which binds to that target, but labwc does not start it
# itself — starting it is opt-in, and this line is the opt-in.
#
# Without it the portals may never start, and screen recording and file dialogs
# quietly do nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
systemctl --user --no-block start labwc-session.target 2>/dev/null || true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2b. Check that it actually happened, and repair it if it did not.
#
# ⚠️ THIS EXISTS BECAUSE OF THE BENCH TEST, 2026-09-03, WHEN THE KEYBOARD
# REMAPPER DID NOT START AT THE FIRST LOGIN AND NOTHING SAID WHY.
#
# `start` above is fire-and-forget: --no-block means "do not wait", and the
# `|| true` means "and do not complain either". Both are right — a login must
# not hang because a target is slow — but between them they turn a failure into
# silence, and silence is what made that evening expensive.
#
# labwc-session.target is BOUND to graphical-session.target, so starting the
# first should start the second, and everything that says "start me with the
# graphical session" then starts too. This checks that the chain really
# happened, says so plainly in the session log either way, and does not simply
# hope.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
aq_tries=0
while [ "${aq_tries}" -lt 20 ]; do
    if systemctl --user is-active graphical-session.target > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        break
    fi
    sleep 0.5
    aq_tries=$((aq_tries + 1))
done

if systemctl --user is-active graphical-session.target > /dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "[session] graphical-session.target is active."
else
    echo "[session] WARNING: graphical-session.target did not start."
    echo "[session] Anything that waits for it — the keyboard remapper, the"
    echo "[session] keyring, some portals — would never run. Starting the"
    echo "[session] keyboard remapper by hand so at least the shortcuts work."
    systemctl --user reset-failed aquarius-keys.service 2>/dev/null || true
    systemctl --user start aquarius-keys.service 2>/dev/null || true
fi >> "${AQ_LOG:-/dev/null}" 2>&1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2c. Make the screen the right SIZE.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS THE 2026-09-03 "EVERYTHING IS TINY" FIX.
#
# labwc starts every monitor at 100%, always. On a 4K screen that makes the
# whole desktop physically tiny — which is exactly what Royce saw on the bench,
# on a 55" 4K monitor that GNOME had been running at 125% for weeks.
#
# The helper works out the right size and applies it. Where it gets the answer
# from, in order: your own `aq display scale` setting, then the scale you
# already chose in GNOME (~/.config/monitors.xml), then the monitor's own
# reported size. The full explanation is in the top of the helper and in
# docs/restart/aquarius-display.md.
#
# It runs AFTER the target above, because a screen that changes size while the
# portals are starting is a screen the portals may measure wrong, and BEFORE
# the shell, because the bar should be drawn once, at the right size, rather
# than drawn small and then jump.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ -x /usr/libexec/aquarius-display-scale ]; then
    /usr/libexec/aquarius-display-scale >> "${AQ_LOG:-/dev/null}" 2>&1 || true
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. The wallpaper.
#
# A window manager on its own paints the screen a flat grey. swaybg puts the
# Aquarius wallpaper behind everything, which matters more than it sounds: the
# difference between "my computer is broken" and "my computer is starting" is
# often just whether the screen looks deliberate.
#
# This is here rather than in the shell because a wallpaper should survive the
# shell crashing. If the bar dies you should still be looking at your desktop.
#
# --mode fill crops to the screen rather than stretching; -c is the colour
# behind the image while it loads, and it is the Midnight void from the boot
# splash so the hand-off from Plymouth has no flash of grey.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ -r /usr/share/backgrounds/aquarius/the-pour-ice-3840x2160.png ]; then
    swaybg -c '#06070C' \
        -i /usr/share/backgrounds/aquarius/the-pour-ice-3840x2160.png \
        -m fill >> "${AQ_LOG:-/dev/null}" 2>&1 &
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. The Aquarius Shell.
#
# Started through a helper rather than directly, for one reason: if the shell
# fails, somebody has to say so on the screen. The helper runs it, logs it, and
# if it dies puts a dialog in front of you explaining what happened and how to
# get out. /usr/libexec/aquarius-shell-start is worth reading — it is short.
#
# If the shell crashes, labwc keeps running and you keep your windows. You get a
# desktop with no bar, which is ugly but survivable, and typing `qs` in a
# terminal brings it back. That is deliberately better than the alternative,
# where a broken bar logs you out — and it is why aquarius-session starts labwc
# without its -S/--session flag, which would tie the window manager's life to
# the shell's.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/usr/libexec/aquarius-shell-start &

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4b. The thing that asks you for your password.
#
# ⚠️ THIS LINE EXISTS BECAUSE OF THE BENCH TEST, 2026-09-04, WHEN PRESSING
#    "INSTALL" IN THE CREATOR-APPS WINDOW FAILED WITH:
#
#      Error creating textual authentication agent: Error opening current
#      controlling terminal for the process ('/dev/tty')
#
# Installing an app for the whole computer needs an administrator, and the part
# of Linux that stops and asks for a password is a small background program
# called a polkit authentication agent. GNOME has one built in. This session had
# none, so `pkexec` tried to ask in a terminal — and there is no terminal behind
# a window you clicked in an app grid.
#
# The helper picks the agent, waits so that a shell with an agent of its own
# always wins the race, and says in this log what it decided. The long version,
# including why "is one already registered?" cannot be asked, is in the header
# of /usr/libexec/aquarius-polkit-agent.
#
# It is started here rather than as a systemd user service on purpose: an agent
# registers itself against A SESSION, and this file is the only place that runs
# once, inside one, with the session's environment already set up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ -x /usr/libexec/aquarius-polkit-agent ]; then
    /usr/libexec/aquarius-polkit-agent >> "${AQ_LOG:-/dev/null}" 2>&1 &
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. The creator apps window, once, on the first login.
#
# ⚠️ THIS LINE EXISTS BECAUSE LABWC DOES NOT READ /etc/xdg/autostart.
#
# On GNOME, a file in /etc/xdg/autostart is all it takes: GNOME reads that
# folder at login and starts everything in it. labwc reads exactly ONE file —
# this one — and nothing else. That is deliberate and it is what keeps a dozen
# GNOME background programs out of the Aquarius session (see the build check
# "Nothing in this session may fight the shell"). It also means anything that
# must run at login in BOTH sessions has to be written down twice: once as a
# .desktop file for GNOME, and once here.
#
# So this line and /etc/xdg/autostart/aquarius-creator-apps-firstrun.desktop are
# the same fact said in two languages. CHANGE ONE, CHANGE BOTH — the build
# checks that both exist and that they run the same command.
#
# --first-run is what makes it "once": the window looks for
# ~/.config/aquarius/creator-apps-seen and returns silently if it is there. So
# this runs at every login and does nothing at all on all but the first.
#
# The ten-second wait matches the X-GNOME-Autostart-Delay in the GNOME copy. It
# is not for us — the window opens in well under a second — it is so that the
# desktop has settled and the person is looking at their computer rather than at
# a login screen when it appears.
#
# THE aquarius-shell REPOSITORY HAS A TWIN OF THIS BLOCK (2026-09-04), in its
# session/labwc/autostart, plus the niri equivalent in session/niri/config.kdl.
# That copy is what runs when somebody starts the session from a clone of the
# shell — on the bench, or on plain Fedora. It is NOT what ships: the image
# build (build_files/stage-aquarius-shell.sh) deliberately leaves that repo's
# session/ folder behind and installs these files instead, because ours are
# adapted to system paths and carry steps that repo has no business knowing
# about (the wallpaper, the display-scale helper, the shell-start dialog).
#
# ⚠️ SO THIS FILE IS THE AUTHORITATIVE ONE, AND THE TWO ARE KEPT IN STEP BY
# HAND. The five lines below are byte-for-byte identical to the shell repo's,
# so a diff between the two files shows only prose. Change one, change the
# other — and the shell repo's own test 17b fails if a login-time line appears
# in one of its two session files and not the other.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ -x /usr/libexec/aquarius-creator-apps ]; then
    (
        sleep 10
        /usr/libexec/aquarius-creator-apps --first-run
    ) >> "${AQ_LOG:-/dev/null}" 2>&1 &
fi
